{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat assignments/T-PROF-split-vs-static.md'","aggregated_output":"# Assignment T-PROF (campaign c2-resume, x86-main; owner Claude Opus; worker Codex) — profile analysis for D030\nQuestion: why is the hybrid expert split (cc/hybrid_split, decisions/D030) 16-32% SLOWER than static layer offload (--n-cpu-moe) at equal GPU MiB, with the deficit growing as more experts are hot?\nInputs (nsys 2024.6.2 graph-level traces, --sample=none --cpuctxsw=none, -t cuda,nvtx,osrt, --cuda-graph-trace=graph; same binary; chat prompt -n 160 decode): artifacts/x4-hs/prof/split-n12.sqlite and static-n12.sqlite (+ .nsys-rep). Note D008: graph-level traces perturb less; node-level not captured. Read decisions/D008, D011, D019, D030 and cc/hybrid_split/README.md (design: per-slot hot dispatch, router GET_ROWS reorder, CPU cold MUL_MAT_ID, 24 layers each with CPU work).\nDo (Python sqlite3 only, light CPU): per decode token (segment by the per-token pattern, discard the first ~5 tokens as warmup): count of CUDA graph launches, kernel launches outside graphs, cudaStreamSynchronize/cudaMemcpy (by kind/size) calls and time, D2H/H2D bytes, GPU busy time, GPU idle gaps (count + total), and wall time per token; top kernels by total time for each; identify whether split runs ANY kernels inside CUDA graphs (graph reuse broken?). Produce a side-by-side table and attribute the per-token difference (split - static) into buckets: extra GPU kernels, extra syncs/transfers, CPU-side time while GPU idle, graph capture loss.\nThen propose (do not implement) the 2-3 most promising redesigns with a predicted per-token saving each, grounded in the numbers (e.g. CUDA MUL_MAT_ID supporting negative/skip IDs to avoid per-slot dispatch; grouping cold work so only K layers touch the CPU; overlapping CPU cold compute with GPU hot compute via async copies).\nDeliver artifacts/codexPROF/ANALYSIS.md + analyze_nsys.py (reusable tool: a profiling CC for \"per-token decode breakdown from nsys sqlite\") + its pytest on a tiny synthetic sqlite. Allowed writes: cc/nsys_decode/** (the tool), artifacts/codexPROF/**. 40 min, no GPU, no Docker, light CPU. Final message: the table, attribution, proposals, tool usage.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
