{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat decisions/D008* decisions/D011* decisions/D019* decisions/D030*; ls -lh artifacts/x4-hs/prof; ls cc'","aggregated_output":"# D008 — Decode profile of Granite BF16 on llama.cpp (build 11723) and first screens\nEvidence: artifacts/profiles/tg128.nsys-rep (graph-level), tg128_node.nsys-rep (node-level), *_kern_sum.csv, lab/jobs/job-faead0556b (screen1).\n- Graph-level trace (less perturbing): per-token GPU graph exec median 1580 us, inter-graph gap median 170 us => ~90% GPU execution, ~10% host gap. Unprofiled decode 599 t/s (A/A spread 0.5-2%).\n- Node-level trace: ~487 kernels/token; ~71% of kernel time in 3 BF16 matmul kernels (mul_mat_vec_f bf16 x2, mul_mat_f bf16). BUT node-level tracing inflated the inter-token gap to ~507 us and graph-launch to ~367 us: PROFILER PERTURBATION. First reading (25% host gap) was wrong; corrected by comparing with graph-level trace. Lesson for methodology CC: cross-check any profiler-derived overhead number against a lower-perturbation trace and unprofiled wall time.\n- Per-token host work seen between graphs: 196,620 B D2H (logits 49155 x f32), ~10 tiny H2D copies + syncs (~70 us), graph launch.\n- Screen1 (runtime env, n=6 attempts, counterbalanced ABAB): ctrl 599.1 t/s; GGML_CUDA_GRAPH_OPT=1 595.8 (-0.55%, not supported); GGML_CUDA_DISABLE_GRAPHS=1 574.1 (-4.2%, graphs help).\n- Next hypotheses: H-quant (representation: Q8_0, Q4_K_M, labelled), H-bw (BF16 matvec kernels reach only part of DRAM bandwidth: needs ncu), H-gap (<=10% ceiling for host-gap work), H-fuse (fewer small kernels).\n# D011 — Bandwidth screen (ncu) and Q8_0 decode profile; engine candidate selected\nEvidence: artifacts/profiles/tg_matmul_ncu.{ncu-rep,csv} (ncu 2025.1.1, SYS_ADMIN, profiling-only timings, NOT inference speed); q8_tg128_{node,graph}.{nsys-rep,sqlite}.\nBF16 (D008): MoE expert matvec (grid 512x8, 16.8 MB, 25 us) = 75% DRAM peak (~665 GB/s, spec peak 896 GB/s is a published value, not measured here); attention projections (1-2 MB, 4-6 us) only 30-44% => latency-bound; mul_mat_f 63%. => BF16 kernel-level headroom small (<~7% overall); weight bytes are the lever (confirmed by Q8_0 +32% decode, D009).\nQ8_0 decode: graph exec median 1171 us + gap 178 us => 741 tok/s (profiled graph-level; unprofiled screen 794). 633 kernels/token (vs 487 BF16): +146 quantize_q8_1/token = 14.4% of kernel time, avg 1.6 us each.\nKernel order in a layer shows 3 separate quantize_q8_1 of the same normed activation before the q, k, v matvecs (artifacts/profiles/q8_tg128_node.sqlite).\nSelected engine candidate C1 (H-fuse-q8): reuse one q8_1 quantization of an activation across consecutive consumers. Prediction: remove ~2 quantize launches/layer (~48/token, ~53 us of 1171) => ~3-4.5% decode. Success criterion (frozen before assessment): >= +2% decode on matched counterbalanced A/B (A/A bound 0.5-2%), outputs bit-identical (mean KLD 0, top-1 100% vs unpatched Q8_0), no PPL change. If <2% or non-identical => rejected, reported as such.\nNot chosen now: BF16 kernel tuning (<=7% ceiling), graph-opt env (rejected D008), fusing norm+quantize (larger change, defer).\n# D019 — hybrid_moe_bench (Opus T-J) result on the D014 prediction (all timings UNSCORED/exploratory; discrete GPU; synthetic activations; replayed routing)\nEvidence: cc/hybrid_expert/{RESULTS.md,results.json,results_L12_single_layer.json}, artifacts/opus-hybrid/, evidence/opus-hybrid.jsonl (ops op-eb0f9a7be446 build 350 s; op-2ea9dea3e2e5 24-layer run 62 s).\n- Correctness: all-hit hybrid == GPU reference and zero-hit hybrid == CPU-all, bitwise, all 24 layers; mixed hit/miss within 1.5x the larger endpoint error vs fp64 (CPU quantizes activations Q8_K, CUDA Q8_1 => backends differ by float noise by construction).\n- Miss cost: 30.6 us marginal per CPU-computed expert + ~15 us fixed per layer with any miss (DRAM-bound ~29 GB/s) -> confirms the 33.3 us calibration (D014). AVX2 Q4_K dot confirmed by disassembly.\n- Serial model within -11%/+6% on 24 layers: K16 measured 2808 us/step vs predicted 2996. SUPPORTED (single-layer-in-isolation timing misleading: L3-resident).\n- Perfect-overlap bound NOT reached (0.06-0.33 of hideable time saved; 25-35 us/layer of CUDA launch work shares the CPU thread): D014's 517-704 t/s upper range is NOT supported by this implementation. Serial form stands: ~330 t/s at ~897 MiB derived (vs 212 measured static n12) — derived, not measured end to end.\n- H2D admission 37.9 us/expert pinned, 90 us pageable ~= CPU compute cost => LRU that admits every miss loses (admission traffic 1853 us/step at K16 > CPU time saved; hit rate 74.4% vs static 73.3%). Static hot set ~ LRU hit rate with zero admission traffic and CUDA-graph friendliness.\n- Existing engine feature found: llama.cpp 31288c9c6 has --moe-cache-mib (uploads misses). Test it end to end (next).\nStatus of H-slotcache: serial prediction supported at single-layer-harness level; end-to-end still unmeasured.\n# D030 — T-R hybrid expert split REJECTED as implemented (slower than static layer offload at equal GPU MiB)\nProtocol D027 (+amendment 1). Job job-d1fb4e9879 (image llamacpp-hsplit1 run_ref sha256:6e91ace0...), same binary for all, 27/27 clean, 3 alternating rounds.\nDecode median t/s (GPU delta MiB): n18: static 149.8 (902) | static+flags 144.5 | split 125.3 (910) = -16%.  n12: 189.8 | 198.8 | split 160.8 (1072) = -19%.  n6: 309.1 | 311.5 | split 211.9 (1234) = -32%. Prefill also lower (split 211-252 vs static 269-322).\nQuality gate not reached (rejected on speed first); CUDA unit-test mismatch still open (amendment 1).\nKey observation: the deficit GROWS as more experts are hot (n18 -> n6), so CPU expert compute is not the bottleneck; candidates: (1) per-slot GPU dispatch (patch issues one MUL_MAT_ID per selected slot to avoid duplicate null IDs in mmid.cu -> up to 8x kernels per projection), (2) 24 GPU<->CPU handoffs/token (every layer has a cold part) vs K for static, (3) router-order GET_ROWS/transposes. Next discriminating experiment: nsys timeline split-n12 vs static-n12 (kernel count/token, sync count, CPU-busy intervals).\nLesson (methodology): single-layer harness predicted ~330 t/s at ~897 MiB (D019); the integrated graph pays per-layer fixed costs the harness did not model. Harness wins are hypotheses until integrated (prompt §13 confirmed).\ntotal 13M\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 muk muk 2.3M Nov  9 23:04 split-n12.nsys-rep\n-rw-r--r-- 1 muk muk 5.8M Nov  9 23:04 split-n12.sqlite\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 muk muk 4.4K Nov  9 23:04 split.log\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 muk muk 1.3M Nov  9 23:04 static-n12.nsys-rep\n-rw-r--r-- 1 muk muk 3.4M Nov  9 23:04 static-n12.sqlite\n-rw-rw-r-- 1 muk muk 4.4K Nov  9 23:04 static.log\nbuild_engine\ndemo\nengine_handoff\ngpu_budget\nhybrid_expert\nhybrid_split\nmemory_pareto\nmodel_inspect\nmoe_planner\nmoe_static_hot\nq8_reuse\nresource_observe\nrouting_trace\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
